;; MPMC Queue using atomic types
;; Demonstrates: atomic types, acq-rel ordering, linear exemption

;; Queue cell: (atomic u32) - an atomic unsigned 32-bit value
;; acq-rel ordering used for push/pop operations

;; Conceptual queue structure (simplified for Prova):
;; - Cell 0: head pointer (atomic u32 acq-rel)
;; - Cell 1: tail pointer (atomic u32 acq-rel)  
;; - Cell 2: data slot 0 (atomic u32)
;; - Cell 3: data slot 1 (atomic u32)

;; Producer function: pushes one item
;; The atomic type on head/tail ensures no data races,
;; while the linear exemption allows both producer and consumer
;; to hold references simultaneously.

;; In a full implementation, would need:
;; - Power-of-2 buffer size for modular arithmetic
;; - Padding between cells for false sharing prevention
;; - Memory barriers beyond acq-rel if needed
;; - Termination detection for consumer

;; This example demonstrates the TYPE SYNTAX and LINEAR EXEMPTION
;; The full algorithm would be implemented in assembly or C
;; where atomic operations are natively supported.

;; For Prova, the key value is that atomic types:
;; 1. Are exempt from linear possession checking
;; 2. Can be shared across functions/threads
;; 3. Support acq-rel, relaxed, acq-cst, rel-cst ordering
;; 4. Emit !volatile memref operations in MLIR

;; Producer function: pushes one item
(fn queue_push
  (params (q (atomic u32)) (data u32) (ok u32))
  (returns u32)
  (pre)
  (post (= ok 1))
  (body
    (do
      ;; Conceptual: atomically store data and advance head
      (return 1)
    )
  )
)

;; Consumer: pops one item
(fn queue_pop
  (params (q (atomic u32))) 
  (returns u32)
  (pre)
  (post)
  (body
    (return 0)
  )
)